<<<
till the expressions meaning “unknown to law’ & ‘ arising from mistake ’ identify them for us; both expressions are therefore of the defining kind, & legitimately linked by and; whether which occurs in both, or only in one, is of no importance. In that example there can, owing to the vagueness of the antecedent things, be no sort of
===
till the expressions meaning ‘unknown to law’ & ‘arising from mistake’ identify them for us; both expressions are therefore of the defining kind, & legitimately linked by *and*; whether *which* occurs in both, or only in one, is of no importance. In that example there can, owing to the vagueness of the antecedent *things*, be no sort of
>>>

<<<
in the Mill sentence, for instance, ‘ another natural prejudice’ is not a vague description like things, demanding
===
in the Mill sentence, for instance, ‘another natural prejudice’ is not a vague description like *things*, demanding
